Consumer request from kubernetes-ocp k259. Files written by File::SOPS 0.002 carry `sops.lastmodified` as a bare YAML timestamp; `sops -d` refuses them, and File::SOPS (>= 0.003) warns when it reads one. The detector behind that warning is private, so OCP (OCP::Secrets->repair_sops_lastmodified) re-implements it as a text match inside the root `sops:` block before rotating the file to rewrite it quoted.

Done when File::SOPS offers a public, documented way to ask whether a file (or its raw text) has the bare form — e.g. a class method returning true/false, without decrypting — so consumers can drop their own match. Optional: a public `repair`/re-quote helper that rewrites only the metadata quoting when the MAC allows it, else documents that rotate is the way.

Settled by the hub session perl-developer-bb for the 2026-11-06 release round, not by the maintainer: a public, documented class method says whether a file or its raw text carries a bare sops.lastmodified; it decrypts nothing and returns true or false; the read-side warning uses the same check; no repair helper -- the POD names rotate as the way to rewrite the file; tests for both forms plus a red run showing the method was missing before; Changes names the method. Chosen by the orchestrator (not a maintainer requirement): name has_bare_lastmodified, named arguments file => PATH or encrypted => TEXT (exactly one), optional format as in decrypt and decrypt_file, returns 1 or 0, never warns. Non-YAML formats answer 0. Lanes in sequence: file-sops-test-writer (red test), file-sops-format (shared predicate in Format::YAML), file-sops-api (public method and POD).

